Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `right_gene_id` (variant_disease_coloc): filter for ENSG00000169174  
• `coloc_h4`, `coloc_h3` (variant_disease_coloc): filter for h4 > 0.8 and h3 < 0.02  
• `coloc_log2_h4_h3` (variant_disease_coloc): measure to summarise (average, variance, range)  
• `right_bio_feature` (variant_disease_coloc): filter for “IPSC”  
• `right_study` (variant_disease_coloc): QTL study that produced the signal – we need the one that owns the maximum log2(h4/h3)  
• `left_chrom`, `left_pos`, `left_ref`, `left_alt` / `right_chrom`, `right_pos`, `right_ref`, `right_alt` (variant_disease_coloc): build the string “1_55029009_C_T” to pin‑down the variant (we do it on the left side; add OR on the right side if required)  
• `study_id`, `trait_reported` (studies): bring the reported GWAS trait so we can keep only rows whose text contains “lesterol levels”  

Typical filter block that will be reused  
```
vc.right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
AND vc.coloc_h4 > 0.8
AND vc.coloc_h3 < 0.02
AND vc.right_bio_feature = 'IPSC'
AND LOWER(s.trait_reported) LIKE '%lesterol levels%'
AND (
      CONCAT(vc.left_chrom,'_',CAST(vc.left_pos AS STRING),'_',vc.left_ref,'_',vc.left_alt) = '1_55029009_C_T'
   OR CONCAT(vc.right_chrom,'_',CAST(vc.right_pos AS STRING),'_',vc.right_ref,'_',vc.right_alt) = '1_55029009_C_T'
    )
```

Below are ten exploratory → analytical BigQuery statements (LIMIT 20 for inspection statements).  
Each query is preceded by a short description.

```sql
-- 1. Quick peek at coloc table
SELECT * 
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct gene IDs present on the right side
SELECT DISTINCT right_gene_id
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. All rows with right_gene_id = ENSG00000169174
SELECT *
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
WHERE right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Add biological‑feature filter (IPSC)
SELECT right_study, coloc_h4, coloc_h3, coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
WHERE right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
  AND right_bio_feature = 'IPSC'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Retain strong‑coloc rows (h4>0.8, h3<0.02)
SELECT right_study, coloc_h4, coloc_h3, coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
WHERE right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
  AND right_bio_feature = 'IPSC'
  AND coloc_h4 > 0.8
  AND coloc_h3 < 0.02
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Inspect the variant string we need, built on the LEFT side
SELECT
  CONCAT(left_chrom,'_',CAST(left_pos AS STRING),'_',left_ref,'_',left_alt) AS variant_id,
  coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
WHERE right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Check if the target variant appears on the RIGHT side as well
SELECT
  CONCAT(right_chrom,'_',CAST(right_pos AS STRING),'_',right_ref,'_',right_alt) AS variant_id,
  coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc`
WHERE right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join to studies table to look at trait_reported
SELECT
  vc.left_study,
  s.trait_reported,
  vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc` AS vc
JOIN `open-targets-genetics.genetics.studies`            AS s
  ON vc.left_study = s.study_id
WHERE vc.right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
  AND vc.coloc_h4 > 0.8
  AND vc.coloc_h3 < 0.02
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Filter on trait text containing 'lesterol levels'
SELECT
  vc.left_study,
  vc.right_study,
  vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc` AS vc
JOIN `open-targets-genetics.genetics.studies`            AS s
  ON vc.left_study = s.study_id
WHERE vc.right_gene_id = 'ENSG00000169174'
  AND vc.coloc_h4 > 0.8
  AND vc.coloc_h3 < 0.02
  AND vc.right_bio_feature = 'IPSC'
  AND LOWER(s.trait_reported) LIKE '%lesterol levels%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final analytical query – average, variance, range, and right_study of maximum log2(h4/h3)
SELECT
  AVG(vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3)                               AS avg_log2_h4_h3,
  VAR_POP(vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3)                           AS var_log2_h4_h3,
  (MAX(vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3) - MIN(vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3))  AS range_log2_h4_h3,
  ARRAY_AGG(
       DISTINCT vc.right_study
       ORDER BY vc.coloc_log2_h4_h3 DESC
       LIMIT 1
  )[OFFSET(0)]                                           AS right_study_of_max
FROM `open-targets-genetics.genetics.variant_disease_coloc` AS vc
JOIN `open-targets-genetics.genetics.studies`              AS s
  ON vc.left_study = s.study_id
WHERE vc.right_gene_id     = 'ENSG00000169174'
  AND vc.coloc_h4          > 0.8
  AND vc.coloc_h3          < 0.02
  AND vc.right_bio_feature = 'IPSC'
  AND LOWER(s.trait_reported) LIKE '%lesterol levels%'
  AND (
        CONCAT(vc.left_chrom,'_',CAST(vc.left_pos AS STRING),'_',vc.left_ref,'_',vc.left_alt) = '1_55029009_C_T'
     OR CONCAT(vc.right_chrom,'_',CAST(vc.right_pos AS STRING),'_',vc.right_ref,'_',vc.right_alt) = '1_55029009_C_T'
      );
```

The sequence shows how to explore the data step‑by‑step and finishes with a single query that delivers  
• average log₂(H4/H3)  
• population variance of log₂(H4/H3)  
• max‑minus‑min difference (range)  
• the QTL source (right_study) that has the highest log₂(H4/H3) under the required filters.
